/-!
# The residual divisor of `ℐ_Y · 𝒪_W` in the blowup at infinity

Let `X` be a scheme and `ℐ_Y ⊆ ℐ_Z` quasi-coherent ideal sheaves on `X`, cutting out closed
subschemes `Z ⊆ Y ⊆ X`. Let `W_Z ⟶ 𝐏¹_X` be the blowup at infinity of Stacks 0FBG in the centre
`∞(Z)`, with exceptional divisor `E_Z`, fibre at infinity `W_{Z,∞}` and structure morphism
`b : W_Z ⟶ X`. The ideal of `∞(Z)` is `ℐ_Z · 𝒪_{𝐏¹_X} + ℐ_{(𝐏¹_X)_∞}`, so the inverse image
`ℐ_Y · 𝒪_{W_Z}` of `ℐ_Y` is contained in the ideal of `E_Z`. As `E_Z` is an effective Cartier
divisor, `ℐ_Y · 𝒪_{W_Z}` is `E_Z` times the colon ideal

  `𝒟_Z = (ℐ_Y · 𝒪_{W_Z} : ℐ_{E_Z})`,

the *residual ideal*, and `𝒟_Z` is the only ideal sheaf with this property. On the chart
`Spec A[s][I/s]` of `W_Z` over an affine open `Spec A ⊆ X`, with `I = ℐ_Z(Spec A)`, it is the
ideal generated by `ℐ_Y(Spec A)/s`. When `ℐ_Y` is an effective Cartier divisor, so is
`ℐ_Y · 𝒪_{W_Z}` (the pullback along the flat `𝐏¹_X ⟶ X` and then along a blowup, Stacks 0809),
and then so is `𝒟_Z`.

For `ℐ_Z = ℐ_Y` the residual ideal is the strict transform of `𝐏¹_Y`; restricted to the normal
cone `C_YX ⊆ E_Y` it cuts out the zero section of the cone: on the chart over `Spec A` it is the
ideal of `Gr_I(A)` generated by the elements of positive degree.

The residual ideals for the two centres are compatible with the change of centre
`ρ : W_Z ∖ X'_Z ⟶ W_Y` of `Stacks.Chow.blowupAtInfinityChangeOfCentre`: along `ρ` the residual
ideal of `ℐ_Y · 𝒪_{W_Y}` pulls back to that of `ℐ_Y · 𝒪_{W_Z}`, because both are what remains of
`ℐ_Y · 𝒪` after cancelling the effective Cartier divisor `W_{Z,∞} ∩ (W_Z ∖ X'_Z)`.

If `X' ⟶ X` is a morphism that factors through `Y` (that is, `ℐ_Y · 𝒪_{X'} = 0`), the residual
ideal pulls back to zero along the comparison `W'_{Z'} ⟶ W_Z` of the blowups at infinity
(`Stacks.Chow.blowupAtInfinityComap`): pulling back `ℐ_Y · 𝒪 = E_Z · 𝒟_Z` gives `0 = E' · 𝒟'`,
and the exceptional divisor `E'` can be cancelled. So the comparison factors through the closed
subscheme `V(𝒟_Z)`. Off the fibre at infinity `E_Z` is the unit ideal, so there `𝒟_Z` is
`ℐ_Y · 𝒪`; and for a closed immersion `Y ⟶ X` the comparison `W_{Z ∩ Y} ⟶ W_Z` is, off the fibre
at infinity, the base change `𝐀¹_Y ⟶ 𝐀¹_X` of `Y ⟶ X`, whose ideal is again `ℐ_Y · 𝒪`.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.Chow.residualIdealAtInfinity` — the residual ideal `𝒟_Z = (ℐ_Y · 𝒪_{W_Z} : ℐ_{E_Z})`.
* `Stacks.Chow.normalConeZeroSectionIdeal` — the ideal of the zero section of the normal cone
  `C_YX`, the residual ideal for `ℐ_Z = ℐ_Y` restricted to `C_YX ⊆ E_Y`.
* `Stacks.Chow.blowupAtInfinityComapToResidual` — the factorization of the comparison
  `W'_{Z'} ⟶ W_Z` through `V(𝒟_Z)`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Chow.comap_blowupAtInfinityToBase_eq_mul_residualIdealAtInfinity` —
  `ℐ_Y · 𝒪_{W_Z} = ℐ_{E_Z} · 𝒟_Z`, and `Stacks.Chow.eq_residualIdealAtInfinity_of_mul_eq`, its
  uniqueness.
* `Stacks.Chow.isEffectiveCartierDivisor_comap_blowupAtInfinityToBase` and
  `Stacks.Chow.isEffectiveCartierDivisor_residualIdealAtInfinity` — for `ℐ_Y` an effective
  Cartier divisor, `ℐ_Y · 𝒪_{W_Z}` and `𝒟_Z` are effective Cartier divisors.
* `Stacks.Chow.comap_residualIdealAtInfinity_changeOfCentre` — compatibility with the change of
  centre.
* `Stacks.Chow.comap_residualIdealAtInfinity_blowupAtInfinityComap` — the residual ideal pulls
  back to zero along `W'_{Z'} ⟶ W_Z` when `X' ⟶ X` factors through `Y`.
* `Stacks.Chow.comap_residualIdealAtInfinity_compl_fibre`,
  `Stacks.Chow.comap_ker_blowupAtInfinityComap_compl_fibre` and
  `Stacks.Chow.comap_residualIdealAtInfinity_compl_fibre_eq_ker` — off the fibre at infinity,
  `𝒟_Z` and the ideal of the closed immersion `W_{Z ∩ Y} ⟶ W_Z` are both `ℐ_Y · 𝒪`.

## Implementation notes

The residual ideal is defined as a colon ideal sheaf, which exists because the ideal of `E_Z` is
finitely generated on affine opens. It is therefore defined for any pair of ideal sheaves
`ℐ_Y`, `ℐ_Z`, and the factorization `ℐ_Y · 𝒪_{W_Z} = ℐ_{E_Z} · 𝒟_Z` holds as soon as
`ℐ_Y ⊆ ℐ_Z`, with no integrality or Cartier hypothesis; `ℐ_Y` being an effective Cartier
divisor is needed only to make `𝒟_Z` one. In particular `ℐ_Y · 𝒪_{W_Z}` is an effective Cartier
divisor for an effective Cartier divisor `ℐ_Y` on an arbitrary scheme `X`: no integrality of `X`
is needed, since `𝐏¹_X ⟶ X` is flat and an effective Cartier divisor pulls back to one along any
blowup.

The comparison `W'_{Z'} ⟶ W_Z` is taken for an arbitrary morphism `X' ⟶ X` along which `ℐ_Y`
pulls back to zero, the strict transform of `𝐏¹_Y` being the case of the closed immersion
`Y ⟶ X`.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0FBG](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0FBG)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0FBH](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0FBH)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0FEB](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0FEB)
